    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/949/cw/en/job/589975/counsellor": {
      "title": "Counsellor",
      "description": "Counsellor Apply now Job no: 589975 Work type: Part-time Location: Warrnambool Categories: Student Services Based at our Warrnambool Campus + hybrid work arrangements Part-time (0.6FTE), continuing opportunity HEW 8 $123,554 - $141,459 + 17% Superannuation (pro rata part-time) About the Role: Working within a student-centred and trauma-informed framework, you will provide accessible, inclusive and culturally responsive mental health support to a diverse student community. You will deliver evidence-based assessment, referral and short-term counselling interventions that promote student wellbeing, enhance mental health literacy, and support positive academic outcomes. Working collaboratively with students, academic and professional staff, and multidisciplinary wellbeing teams, you will contribute to a mentally healthy learning environment and support continuous improvement initiatives across the University. As a Counsellor, you will: Deliver evidence-based mental health assessments, referrals and short-term counselling interventions to support student wellbeing and success. Manage a range of student mental health presentations, including complex and risk-related concerns, using a student-centred and trauma-informed approach. Collaborate with students, academic staff and multidisciplinary wellbeing teams to provide coordinated support and improve student outcomes. Develop and deliver wellbeing initiatives, workshops and mental health promotion activities that strengthen mental health literacy across the University. Contribute to service excellence through continuous improvement activities, critical incident response, clinical record management, and the provision of specialist mental health advice and consultation. To be successful, you will have: Postgraduate qualifications in Psychology, Social Work or a related discipline, or progress towards postgraduate qualifications with extensive relevant experience, or an equivalent combination of relevant experience and education. Registration as a Psychologist with the Australian Health Practitioner Regulation Agency (AHPRA) or membership of the Australian Association of Social Work (AASW). Demonstrated experience providing evidence-based mental health assessment, risk assessment and short-term counselling interventions. An understanding of the higher education environment and the wellbeing challenges faced by diverse student populations. Strong stakeholder engagement and communication skills, with the ability to work collaboratively across multidisciplinary teams. Experience contributing to service improvement initiatives and delivering high-quality, client-centred wellbeing services. Here's how to apply: Please submit your updated resume and a short cover letter outlining your skills and experience. For a confidential discussion regarding this role, please contact Nicole Pluim, Senior Manager, Student Psychological Programs and Services via nicole.pluim@deakin.edu.au For a copy of the position description, please see below: Counsellor_HEW 8.pdf Applications for this position close on Wednesday 28 October 2026. Benefits for working with us! At Deakin, we offer more than just a job - we offer a lifestyle. Across our campuses in Geelong, Melbourne, and regional Victoria, you’ll find everything you need to thrive: on-site childcare, gyms, medical centres, cafes, accessible public transport, and convenient parking. Enjoy flexible working arrangements that support your work-life balance, generous leave entitlements including extended parental leave and the option to purchase additional time off, plus a wide range of staff discounts on travel and more. Our health and wellbeing services, ongoing professional development opportunities, and inclusive, supportive culture make Deakin a place where you can grow - whether you're working remotely or immersed in the vibrant energy of campus life. Are You Ready? Deakin is a Victorian university with a global impact. We are an agile, dynamic, and innovative university committed to making a positive impact through our excellence in education, research and innovation and the contributions we make to the wider community. We understand that our reputation has been built on the dedication and expertise of our staff and we offer a dynamic and diverse working environment with opportunities to grow and develop careers. We believe that a progressive, thriving culture will ensure that people choose to come, and stay at Deakin and contribute to our ongoing success. We value diversity and aim to build an inclusive environment that champions, embraces and respects differences. We support and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, and people of all abilities, cultures, sexual orientation, and genders. Pre-employment safety and suitability checks: In accordance with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence, appointment to this role is subject to successful completion of relevant pre-employment checks, including Working With Children Checks and candidate gender-based violence declarations. Candidates may be asked to declare whether they have been investigated for an allegation of Gender‑based Violence, or determined to have engaged in conduct that constitutes Gender‑based Violence during the course of their previous employment, or otherwise in a legal process. A declaration is not required from individuals who have experienced gender‑based violence. A ‘Yes’ response does not automatically exclude a candidate from employment, and any information provided will be treated confidentially and considered only for relevance to the role and the University’s safety obligations. Advertised: 14 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 28 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/949/cw/en/job/590334/assistant-manager-institutional-alliance": {
      "title": "Assistant Manager, Institutional Alliance",
      "description": "Assistant Manager, Institutional Alliance Apply now Job no: 590334 Work type: Full-time Location: GIFT City Categories: International Education Based at our GIFT City Campus, India Full-time and continuing Attractive salary package Why work with us? At Deakin you will have access to benefits such as a variety of leave options including generous parental leave and the ability to purchase additional leave; flexible working arrangements to help manage your work-life balance; ongoing learning and development opportunities to grow your career; an inclusive and supportive culture and environment to work in, both online and on campus. About Gift City Deakin is the first University in the world to open an international teaching campus in India. The state-of-the-art campus opened in 2024 in the heart of the smart business district, GIFT City (Gujarat International Finance Tec-City). This campus is giving students access to future-ready Deakin postgraduate courses aligned with local employment needs since July 2024. The programs here will directly support Deakin’s aspiration for innovative international education partnerships that provide lasting value for students, partners, and Deakin. With an ‘In India, with India and for India’ approach, the initiative will also provide opportunities for Deakin and the students to work closely with GIFT City and its industry partners. As an Assistant Manager, Institutional Alliance you will: Build and strengthen institutional partnerships, implementing strategic alliances that support and enhance student recruitment outcomes Develop and manage relationships with schools, universities, pathway providers, coaching centres and corporate partners Coordinate workshops, seminars, outreach programs, campus visits, articulation pathways and MoUs. Monitor partnership performance against recruitment targets and identify new market opportunities through research and trend analysis. Represent the University externally, maintain stakeholder networks and establish systems and processes to support data-driven decision-making and effective partnership management. To be successful, you’ll have: Postgraduate qualifications or extensive relevant experience and management expertise; or equivalent combination of experience and/or education/training Experience conceptualising, developing, reviewing and executing marketing projects in complex environments Experience developing and delivering marketing and communications plans for a wide variety of audiences Experience implementing strategy across key marketing channels, including both print and digital advertising and social marketing channels Sound knowledge of web-based tools and the Microsoft Office package Here's how to apply: Please submit your updated resume and a short cover letter outlining your skills and experience. For a copy of the position description, please see below: PD - Assistant Manager, Institutional Alliance, 590334.pdf We will be actively monitoring CVs so please apply quickly! Please note: Indian Working rights required. Are You Ready? Deakin is a Victorian university with a global impact. We are an agile, dynamic, and innovative university committed to making a positive impact through our excellence in education, research and innovation and the contributions we make to the wider community. We understand that our reputation has been built on the dedication and expertise of our staff and we offer a dynamic and diverse working environment with opportunities to grow and develop careers. We believe that a progressive, thriving culture will ensure that people choose to come, and stay at Deakin and contribute to our ongoing success. We value diversity and aim to build an inclusive environment that champions, embraces and respects differences. We support and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, and people of all abilities, cultures, sexual orientation, and genders. Pre-employment safety and suitability checks: In accordance with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence, appointment to this role is subject to successful completion of relevant pre-employment checks, including Working With Children Checks and candidate gender-based violence declarations. Candidates may be asked to declare whether they have been investigated for an allegation of Gender‑based Violence, or determined to have engaged in conduct that constitutes Gender‑based Violence during the course of their previous employment, or otherwise in a legal process. A declaration is not required from individuals who have experienced gender‑based violence. A ‘Yes’ response does not automatically exclude a candidate from employment, and any information provided will be treated confidentially and considered only for relevance to the role and the University’s safety obligations. Advertised: 27 Oct 2026 India Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/949/cw/en/job/590194/senior-adviser-student-success-learning": {
      "title": "Senior Adviser, Student Success (Learning)",
      "description": "Senior Adviser, Student Success (Learning) Apply now Job no: 590194 Work type: Full-time Location: Geelong - City, Geelong - Waurn Ponds, Melbourne - Burwood Categories: Administration, Student Services Flexible Location: Based at our Geelong (Waurn Ponds or Waterfront) or Burwood Campus + hybrid work arrangements Full-time and continuing HEW 7 $110,171 - $121,309 + 17% Superannuation About the role: As the Senior Adviser, Student Success (Learning), you will play a key role in enhancing the student experience and supporting academic success across the University. Working directly with students, academic staff and key stakeholders, you'll design, deliver and continuously improve programs, resources and initiatives that build student capability, foster engagement and contribute to positive learning outcomes. You will lead activities across academic support, peer learning and student partnership initiatives, using data, feedback and insights to ensure services remain responsive to the evolving needs of students. In this role, you will: Deliver initiatives that enhance student learning, engagement and success. Coordinate peer mentoring and academic support programs across the University. Develop resources, workshops and targeted interventions that improve student outcomes. Use data, insights and feedback to evaluate services and drive continuous improvement. Collaborate with students, academic staff and stakeholders to create impactful support initiatives. About you: You are passionate about supporting student success and thrive in a collaborative, student-centred environment. A confident relationship builder, you can translate insights into action, influence stakeholders and navigate complexity with ease. You enjoy improving services, have a natural ability to engage others and are motivated by opportunities to make a tangible difference in students' educational experiences. You will bring: Relevant qualifications and experience, or an equivalent combination of education and expertise. Experience delivering student success, learning support or engagement initiatives. Strong analytical, problem-solving and continuous improvement skills. Demonstrated ability to influence, collaborate and build effective partnerships. Excellent communication and project coordination skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities. Here's how to apply: Please submit your updated resume and a short cover letter outlining your skills and experience. For a confidential discussion regarding this role, please contact Nara Tsedendamba, Specialist, Academic Engagement via nara.tsedendamba@deakin.edu.au For a copy of the position description, please see below: Senior Adviser, Student Success (Learning) HEW 7.pdf Applications for this position close at 11.55pm on Tuesday 10th November. Benefits for working with us! At Deakin, we offer more than just a job - we offer a lifestyle. Across our campuses in Geelong, Melbourne, and regional Victoria, you’ll find everything you need to thrive: on-site childcare, gyms, medical centres, cafes, accessible public transport, and convenient parking. Enjoy flexible working arrangements that support your work-life balance, generous leave entitlements including extended parental leave and the option to purchase additional time off, plus a wide range of staff discounts on travel and more. Our health and wellbeing services, ongoing professional development opportunities, and inclusive, supportive culture make Deakin a place where you can grow - whether you're working remotely or immersed in the vibrant energy of campus life. Are You Ready? Deakin is a Victorian university with a global impact. We are an agile, dynamic, and innovative university committed to making a positive impact through our excellence in education, research and innovation and the contributions we make to the wider community. We understand that our reputation has been built on the dedication and expertise of our staff and we offer a dynamic and diverse working environment with opportunities to grow and develop careers. We believe that a progressive, thriving culture will ensure that people choose to come, and stay at Deakin and contribute to our ongoing success. We value diversity and aim to build an inclusive environment that champions, embraces and respects differences. We support and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, and people of all abilities, cultures, sexual orientation, and genders. Pre-employment safety and suitability checks: In accordance with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence, appointment to this role is subject to successful completion of relevant pre-employment checks, including Working With Children Checks and candidate gender-based violence declarations. Candidates may be asked to declare whether they have been investigated for an allegation of Gender‑based Violence, or determined to have engaged in conduct that constitutes Gender‑based Violence during the course of their previous employment, or otherwise in a legal process. A declaration is not required from individuals who have experienced gender‑based violence. A ‘Yes’ response does not automatically exclude a candidate from employment, and any information provided will be treated confidentially and considered only for relevance to the role and the University’s safety obligations. Advertised: 27 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 10 Nov 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/949/cw/en/job/589879/biological-technical-officer": {
      "title": "Biological Technical Officer",
      "description": "Biological Technical Officer Apply now Job no: 589879 Work type: Full-time Location: Melbourne - Burwood Categories: Technical Services Based at our Melbourne, Burwood Campus Full-time and continuing $86,744 - $97,290 (HEW 5) + 17% superannuation Make an Impact: Unlock hands-on discovery - preparing labs where students build the practical skills that define a biologist. Make science real by turning lectures into working experiments students can see, touch and understand. Protect people and the planet through expert safety and chemical handling that keeps everyone secure every single day. Fuel research by keeping equipment running and materials ready so scientists can focus on breakthroughs, not logistics. Shape future biologists by collaborating with teachers to design practicals that inspire confidence and curiosity in every student. Why you will love this Role: You will work with cutting-edge equipment and instrumentation, building technical expertise in microscopy, analytical tools and specialised lab systems that boost your career prospects. You're part of a collaborative, supportive team that values your contribution - working alongside passionate academics and dedicated colleagues who respect technical expertise. You gain genuine job security and stability in a university setting with clear career development pathways and opportunities to grow your skills over time. You see the direct impact of your work every day - watching students succeed in practicals you've prepared and knowing you've made their learning possible. You master diverse skills across botany, chemistry, molecular biology and general lab practice, making you highly versatile and increasingly valuable in any lab environment. In a typical day, you will: Fire up the lab - prepping specimens, calibrating gear and mixing reagents so students can dive straight into discovery. Be the troubleshooter - fix equipment glitches and coach students through techniques that actually work, live in the lab. Keep the prep room humming - restocking kits, nailing safety records and staying one step ahead of chaos. Team up with lecturers to crack experiments that aren't cooperating, then test your fixes before rolling them out. Tackle real challenges daily - from dodging contamination to mastering chemical safety like a pro. Your New Team Awaits You'll work with award-winning teaching staff who collaborate openly and genuinely value your technical expertise. You're part of a tight-knit team in the School of Life and Environmental Sciences where colleagues support each other and celebrate wins together. The team embraces a hands-on approach - you'll contribute ideas and help shape how labs evolve, not just follow procedures. You'll join a supportive environment where teamwork thrives and your contributions to student success are recognised and appreciated. You may be a great fit for this role if you… Hold a tertiary qualification in biological sciences or laboratory technology with solid chemistry expertise, enabling you to prepare solutions, buffers and reagents with precision across general, molecular and specialized practicals. You bring demonstrated dissection skills and practical knowledge of safe specimen preparation, handling, storage and disposal - essential for supporting dissection-based classes and guiding students safely through complex techniques. Your molecular biology background, combined with hands-on laboratory experience, allows you to operate specialised instrumentation, prepare media and maintain contamination-control procedures that keep molecular practicals running smoothly. You're familiar with university systems and protocols - understanding laboratory safety, risk assessment, hazardous substances, waste management and compliance requirements that govern tertiary teaching labs. You've supported undergraduate biology teaching laboratories through full practical cycles (prep, delivery, pack-down), with strong time management and the ability to juggle competing teaching demands while working independently or collaboratively. Here's how to apply: Please submit your current resume and a short covering letter outlining your skills and experience. Want to Know More? For a confidential discussion, or for further questions regarding this role, please contact Cuong Huynh (Technical Services Coordinator, Life Sciences) via cuong.huynh@deakin.edu.au For a copy of the position description, please see below: PD - Technical Officer 589879.pdf Applications for this position close at 11.55pm on 9 November. Please note that only candidates with full working rights in Australia will be considered for this position. Deakin at a Glance Deakin is a globally recognised university committed to making a positive impact through education, research and innovation. Our success is driven by our people, and we're dedicated to creating a workplace where individuals feel supported, valued and empowered to do their best work. We offer a collaborative and inclusive culture, opportunities for professional growth, and the flexibility to balance work and life. We welcome applications from people of all backgrounds and are committed to building a diverse workforce that reflects the communities we serve. What we Offer At Deakin , we offer more than just a job - we offer a lifestyle. Across our campuses in Geelong, Melbourne, and regional Victoria, you’ll find everything you need to thrive: on-site childcare, gyms, medical centres, cafes, accessible public transport, and convenient parking. Enjoy flexible working arrangements that support your work-life balance, generous leave entitlements including extended parental leave plus a wide range of staff discounts on travel and more. Our health and wellbeing services, ongoing professional development opportunities, and inclusive, supportive culture make Deakin a place where you can grow - whether you're working remotely or immersed in the vibrant energy of campus life. To find out why our staff love working with Deakin, read more here ! Pre-employment safety and suitability checks: In accordance with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence, appointment to this role is subject to successful completion of relevant pre-employment checks, including Working with Children Checks and candidate gender-based violence declarations. As part of Deakin's commitment to providing a safe, respectful and inclusive environment, successful candidates will be required to complete relevant pre-employment checks, which may include a Working with Children Check and declarations relating to gender-based violence in accordance with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence. Any information provided will be treated confidentially and assessed only where relevant to the requirements of the role and Deakin's duty of care obligations. A declaration does not automatically preclude a candidate from employment. Advertised: 26 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 09 Nov 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/949/cw/en/job/590141/senior-officer-simulation-centre": {
      "title": "Senior Officer, Simulation Centre",
      "description": "Senior Officer, Simulation Centre Apply now Job no: 590141 Work type: Full-time Location: Warrnambool Categories: Administration, Technical Services Warrnambool Campus Full-time and continuing HEW 6 $100,128 - $108,130 + 17% Superannuation Benefits for working with us! At Deakin, we offer more than just a job - we offer a lifestyle. Across our campuses in Geelong, Melbourne, and regional Victoria, you’ll find everything you need to thrive: on-site childcare, gyms, medical centres, cafes, accessible public transport, and convenient parking. Enjoy generous leave entitlements including extended parental leave and the option to purchase additional time off, plus a wide range of staff discounts on travel and more. Our health and wellbeing services, ongoing professional development opportunities, and inclusive, supportive culture make Deakin a place where you can grow - whether you're working remotely or immersed in the vibrant energy of campus life. About the Role The Senior Simulation Officer provides high-level technical and operational support for the delivery of simulation-centre based teaching within the School of Nursing and Midwifery. Operating under limited direction, the role is responsible for the planning, coordination, and delivery of teaching in the simulation centre, ensuring that teaching environments are set up according to teaching requirements, are safe, and aligned with academic requirements. The Simulation Centre Officer position is based in the Nursing and Midwifery Simulation Centre within the new Health Precinct at Deakin’s Warrnambool Campus. As a Senior Simulation Officer, you will: Prepare Simulation Centre, including high fidelity suite, for timetabled practical classes, ensuring rooms are set up according to the academic staff requirements and instructions, and all equipment and consumable items are available Regularly assess consumable stock holdings and purchase, as required, appropriate equipment and consumable supplies required for nursing and midwifery simulation classes, as per University Finance process Run scenarios for the high-fidelity simulation clinical skills program as directed by Academic Unit Team staff with Simulation Centre Team leader Maintain and schedule servicing of specialised equipment as required manner complying with OHS policies and instruct staff and students on School OHS procedures Take personal responsibility and show initiative to meeting goals and objectives contributing to an inclusive team environment Actively participate and contribute to the University and Faculty OHS committees and coordinate School OHS inspections to meet University policy timelines To be successful, you’ll have: Degree with subsequent relevant experience; or extensive experience and specialist knowledge; or equivalent combination of experience and/or education/training Experience in working in a Simulation Centre or Healthcare organisation High-level computer skills, particularly Microsoft applications. Experience providing services and support in a large organisation. Experience providing professional advice and delivering outcomes Here's how to apply: Please submit your updated resume and a short cover letter outlining your skills and experience. For a confidential discussion regarding this role, please contact Rosie Rose (Team Leader Simulation Centre) on r.rose@deakin.edu.au For a copy of the position description, please see below: PD-Senior Simulation Officer-590141.pdf Applications for this position close on 8th November 2026 Are You Ready? Deakin is a Victorian university with a global impact. We are an agile, dynamic, and innovative university committed to making a positive impact through our excellence in education, research and innovation and the contributions we make to the wider community. We understand that our reputation has been built on the dedication and expertise of our staff and we offer a dynamic and diverse working environment with opportunities to grow and develop careers. We believe that a progressive, thriving culture will ensure that people choose to come, and stay at Deakin and contribute to our ongoing success. We value diversity and aim to build an inclusive environment that champions, embraces and respects differences. We support and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, and people of all abilities, cultures, sexual orientation, and genders. Pre-employment safety and suitability checks: In accordance with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence, appointment to this role is subject to successful completion of relevant pre-employment checks, including Working With Children Checks and candidate gender-based violence declarations. Candidates may be asked to declare whether they have been investigated for an allegation of Gender‑based Violence, or determined to have engaged in conduct that constitutes Gender‑based Violence during the course of their previous employment, or otherwise in a legal process. A declaration is not required from individuals who have experienced gender‑based violence. A ‘Yes’ response does not automatically exclude a candidate from employment, and any information provided will be treated confidentially and considered only for relevance to the role and the University’s safety obligations. Advertised: 26 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/949/cw/en/job/590207/postdoctoral-research-fellow": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow Apply now Job no: 590207 Work type: Full-time Location: Melbourne - Burwood Categories: Research Based at our Melbourne, Burwood Campus with travel across rural Victoria required Full-time and fixed-term until October 2028 Level A $86,599 - $116,417 + 17% Superannuation Benefits for working with us! At Deakin, we offer more than just a job - we offer a lifestyle. Across our campuses in Geelong, Melbourne, and regional Victoria, you’ll find everything you need to thrive: on-site childcare, gyms, medical centres, cafes, accessible public transport, and convenient parking. Enjoy flexible working arrangements that support your work-life balance, generous leave entitlements including extended parental leave and the option to purchase additional time off, plus a wide range of staff discounts on travel and more. Our health and wellbeing services, ongoing professional development opportunities, and inclusive, supportive culture make Deakin a place where you can grow - whether you're working remotely or immersed in the vibrant energy of campus life. About the Role The Postdoctoral Research Fellow will engage collaboratively to conduct high-quality research, scholarship and creative activities that generate high-impact outcomes. With mentorship from senior researchers and leaders within the research team, the successful candidate will contribute to innovative research projects, support researcher development, and help deliver meaningful real-world impact. The ideal candidate is an applied microeconomist with expertise in behavioural measurement and strong laboratory experiment skills applied to policy issues. The successful candidate will play a substantive intellectual role in refining the RCT and will have the opportunity to co-author high-quality academic publications. Working closely with the Chief Investigators and project partners, the Associate Postdoctoral Research Fellow will take substantial day-to-day responsibility for experimental design, survey and behavioural measure development, farmer recruitment, regional workshops, field team coordination, research ethics, data quality, causal analysis, and the preparation of academic publications and policy outputs. To achieve this, the successful candidate will be required to spend a significant amount of time travelling to regional Victorian locations. As a Postdoctoral Research Fellow, you will: Conduct high-quality research, scholarship and creative activities generating high impact outputs, with mentorship Engage collaboratively to participate in novel and high-quality research or creative activities Ensure impact of academic activity on the field and the community Support and may lead applications for funding for research and creative activities Contribute to Deakin community and its effectiveness via local engagement and citizenship activities To be successful, you’ll have: A PhD awarded or submitted by the time of appointment, in economics, agricultural or resource economics, behavioural or experimental economics, development economics, or environmental economics. Strong knowledge of behavioural and experimental research methods, including lab-in-the-field experiments, design of survey or experimental measures relating to decision-making under risk, intertemporal choice, cooperation, social preferences, or related behavioural outcomes. Advanced applied econometric and causal-inference skills, together with demonstrated capacity to undertake transparent and reproducible analysis using at least one relevant statistical package, such as R or STATA. Evidence of strong research and writing potential relative to career stage, demonstrated through peer-reviewed publications, high-quality working papers, or other relevant outputs, and the ability to communicate findings to both academic and non-academic audiences. Understanding of human-research ethics, informed consent, privacy, research integrity, and responsible data management, with the capacity to contribute to ethics applications, trial registration, pre-analysis plans, project reporting, registered reports for publication, and research documentation. Capacity and willingness to undertake regular travel throughout regional and rural Victoria, including occasional overnight stays and workshops held at times convenient for farming communities. Here's how to apply: Please submit your updated resume, a short cover letter and responses to the Key Selection Criteria. For a confidential discussion regarding this role, please contact Pallavi Shukla (Senior Lecturer) on p.shukla@deakin.edu.au For a copy of the position description, please see below: PD-Postdoctoral Research Fellow-590207.pdf Applications for this position will close on Tuesday, 3rd November 2026. Are You Ready? Deakin is a Victorian university with a global impact. We are an agile, dynamic, and innovative university committed to making a positive impact through our excellence in education, research and innovation and the contributions we make to the wider community. We understand that our reputation has been built on the dedication and expertise of our staff and we offer a dynamic and diverse working environment with opportunities to grow and develop careers. We believe that a progressive, thriving culture will ensure that people choose to come, and stay at Deakin and contribute to our ongoing success. We value diversity and aim to build an inclusive environment that champions, embraces and respects differences. We support and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, and people of all abilities, cultures, sexual orientation, and genders. Pre-employment safety and suitability checks: In accordance with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence, appointment to this role is subject to successful completion of relevant pre-employment checks, including Working With Children Checks and candidate gender-based violence declarations. Candidates may be asked to declare whether they have been investigated for an allegation of Gender‑based Violence, or determined to have engaged in conduct that constitutes Gender‑based Violence during the course of their previous employment, or otherwise in a legal process. A declaration is not required from individuals who have experienced gender‑based violence. A ‘Yes’ response does not automatically exclude a candidate from employment, and any information provided will be treated confidentially and considered only for relevance to the role and the University’s safety obligations. Advertised: 23 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/949/cw/en/job/589512/lecturer-in-health-promotion-educationfocused": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Health Promotion, Education-Focused",
      "description": "Lecturer in Health Promotion, Education-Focused Apply now Job no: 589512 Work type: Education-focused Academic, Part-time Location: Melbourne - Burwood Categories: Health Based at our Melbourne, Burwood Campus Part-time (0.5 FTE) and continuing in the School of Health & Social Development $122,391 - $144,759 (Level B) + 17% Superannuation (pro rata, part time) The Opportunity The Faculty of Health is dedicated to being at the forefront of teaching, learning, creativity, and groundbreaking research and discovery. Working closely with industry partners and government, the Faculty translates research and innovation into real-world impact. Within the Faculty, the School of Health and Social Development places a strong emphasis on education and research that is solution-focused and makes a meaningful difference to individuals, communities, and society. We work closely with industry partners and government to ensure our teaching and research address contemporary challenges and contribute to positive social outcomes. The Education-focused Academic will lead unit teaching teams across undergraduate and postgraduate health promotion programs, contributing to exceptional student learning experiences and outcomes. The role will initiate, design, and deliver high-quality scholarly and educational activities that enhance teaching practice, curriculum innovation, student success, and graduate employability. Working collaboratively with colleagues and external partners, the position will contribute to strengthening the School's engagement with the health sector through teaching, learning, and community partnerships. This role is education-focused and will contribute to teaching and learning in the health promotion program. This position is suited to a person with an excellent record and passion for scholarly learning and teaching and a keen interest in exploring and building on new areas of educational development. The successful candidate will contribute to community engagement with the health sector through teaching, learning, and employability-focused initiatives, helping to enhance student outcomes and strengthen connections between the University and industry. As a Lecturer, Education-Focused, you will: Lead unit teaching teams and work collaboratively with other unit teaching teams in the course to develop effective unit curricula and learning resources (including through unit leadership roles). Contribute to the review of units and courses and implement refinements. Assist in applying industry, clinical and/or research practice into student learning experiences, success, engagement and resources. Adopt new innovation in curriculum design at the unit level and deliver learner-centric, research-informed teaching practices across different levels / modes of delivery. Maintain deep discipline knowledge which is applied to inform unit design. Design collaborative learning opportunities at the unit level. Support diverse learners by designing accessible and inclusive unit material and practices. Make a significant contribution to pedagogy research projects and demonstrate a disciplinary reputation in teaching scholarship and pedagogical research. Make significant advances in knowledge within the specific fields/areas of teaching scholarship and pedagogical research activity in the scholarship of teaching and educational development through independent contribution to pedagogical research activity. To be successful, you’ll meet the following Key Selection Criteria: Completion or near completion or enrolment for a doctoral qualification in a relevant field or an equivalent accreditation and standing Excellent record of scholarly learning and teaching in UG and/or PG programs. Capacity to contribute to innovative curriculum design. Capacity to contribute to research and scholarship in a health-related discipline through publications and/or external research grants. Ability to contribute to communities through teaching and/or research. Capacity to contribute to leadership of teaching programs. Excellent interpersonal skills and a proven ability to establish good working relationships with colleagues. Registration, or eligible for registration, with the International Union for Health Promotion and Education preferred but not essential Demonstrated passion for teaching and learning. Ability to deliver effective teaching practices to improve student success and engagement. Here's how to apply: Applicants must have full Australian working rights. Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Please submit your updated resume, a short cover letter and responses to the Key Selection Criteria. For a confidential discussion regarding this role, please contact Matthew Dunn (Associate Professor) on m.dunn@deakin.edu.au . For a copy of the position description, please see below: PD - Level B_Education-Focused 589512.pdf Applications for this position close on Thursday 12th November at 11:55pm. Interviews are expected to be held during the week commencing 23rd November. Benefits for working with us! At Deakin, we offer more than just a job. We offer a rewarding career in a flexible, inclusive and forward-thinking environment. Across our campuses in Geelong, Melbourne and regional Victoria, you'll enjoy flexible working arrangements that support work-life balance, generous leave provisions including extended parental leave and the option to purchase additional leave, and access to a range of professional development opportunities to help you grow your career. Are You Ready? Deakin is a Victorian university with a global impact. We are an agile, dynamic, and innovative university committed to making a positive impact through our excellence in education, research and innovation and the contributions we make to the wider community. We understand that our reputation has been built on the dedication and expertise of our staff and we offer a dynamic and diverse working environment with opportunities to grow and develop careers. We believe that a progressive, thriving culture will ensure that people choose to come, and stay at Deakin and contribute to our ongoing success. We value diversity and aim to build an inclusive environment that champions, embraces and respects differences. We support and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, and people of all abilities, cultures, sexual orientation, and genders. Pre-employment safety and suitability checks: In accordance with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence, appointment to this role is subject to successful completion of relevant pre-employment checks, including Working With Children Checks and candidate gender-based violence declarations. Candidates may be asked to declare whether they have been investigated for an allegation of Gender‑based Violence, or determined to have engaged in conduct that constitutes Gender‑based Violence during the course of their previous employment, or otherwise in a legal process. A declaration is not required from individuals who have experienced gender‑based violence. A ‘Yes’ response does not automatically exclude a candidate from employment, and any information provided will be treated confidentially and considered only for relevance to the role and the University’s safety obligations. Advertised: 22 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/949/cw/en/job/590112/senior-international-student-support-officer": {
      "title": "Senior International Student Support Officer",
      "description": "Senior International Student Support Officer Apply now Job no: 590112 Work type: Fixed-term, Full-time Location: Melbourne - Burwood Categories: Administration, Student Services, Customer Service Based at our Melbourne, Burwood Campus + hybrid work arrangements Full-time and fixed-term until January 2028 HEW 6 $100,128 - $108,130 + 17% Superannuation Position Overview The International Student Support (ISS) team act as an escalation point to provide advice and support regarding complex issues, including health and wellbeing support and academic progress for international students. The ISS team ensures that the University meets its legislative welfare, safety, and support obligations under the ESOS Act 2000, The National Code of Practice, the Migration Act, and other State and National laws and standards in force at any time Benefits for working with us! At Deakin, we offer more than just a job - we offer a lifestyle. Across our campuses in Geelong, Melbourne, and regional Victoria, you’ll find everything you need to thrive: on-site childcare, gyms, medical centres, cafes, accessible public transport, and convenient parking. Enjoy flexible working arrangements that support your work-life balance, generous leave entitlements including extended parental leave and the option to purchase additional time off, plus a wide range of staff discounts on travel and more. Our health and wellbeing services, ongoing professional development opportunities, and inclusive, supportive culture make Deakin a place where you can grow - whether you're working remotely or immersed in the vibrant energy of campus life. Who are we? Deakin is a cutting-edge public university headquartered in Victoria, revolutionising education with 61,000 students across our campuses: Melbourne Burwood, Geelong Waurn Ponds, Geelong Waterfront, Warrnambool, Deakin University Lancaster University Indonesia (DLI), GIFT City, India, and our vibrant online environment. We're proud to be a progressive and open-minded university, delivering the highest student satisfaction in Victoria and consistently ranked in the top 1% of the world's universities. As a Senior International Student Support Officer, you will: Plan and prioritise work and critical activities, recognising barriers and identifying effective ways to address them while evaluating progress and adjusting approach Analyse information from a range of sources and distil key issues to support decision-making Build productive stakeholder relationships and communicate with confidence and clarity Contribute to the planning, implementation and evaluation of services and programs to enhance the international student experience throughout their lifecycle with the University Contribute in pre-departure orientation sessions and assists in delivering integrated orientation and engagement initiatives and programs, with a focus on intercultural engagement To be successful, you’ll have: Degree with subsequent relevant experience; or extensive experience and specialist knowledge; or equivalent combination of experience and/or education/training Experience providing professional advice and delivering outcomes Ability to manage competing priorities Strong analytical and problem-solving capability Sound knowledge of relevant policies and governance, in particular ESOS Act 2018 for international students and students Under 18 years of age and of the Victorian Child Safe Standards and Australia Awards policies Here's how to apply: Please submit your updated resume and a short cover letter outlining your skills and experience. For a confidential discussion regarding this role, please contact John Brown (Manager International Student Support and Compliance) on john.dean@deakin.edu.au For a copy of the position description, please see below: PD - Senior International Student Support Officer 590112.pdf Applications for this position close on 8 November 2026. Are You Ready? Deakin is a Victorian university with a global impact. We are an agile, dynamic, and innovative university committed to making a positive impact through our excellence in education, research and innovation and the contributions we make to the wider community. We understand that our reputation has been built on the dedication and expertise of our staff and we offer a dynamic and diverse working environment with opportunities to grow and develop careers. We believe that a progressive, thriving culture will ensure that people choose to come, and stay at Deakin and contribute to our ongoing success. We value diversity and aim to build an inclusive environment that champions, embraces and respects differences. We support and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, and people of all abilities, cultures, sexual orientation, and genders. Pre-employment safety and suitability checks: In accordance with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence, appointment to this role is subject to successful completion of relevant pre-employment checks, including Working With Children Checks and candidate gender-based violence declarations. Candidates may be asked to declare whether they have been investigated for an allegation of Gender‑based Violence, or determined to have engaged in conduct that constitutes Gender‑based Violence during the course of their previous employment, or otherwise in a legal process. A declaration is not required from individuals who have experienced gender‑based violence. A ‘Yes’ response does not automatically exclude a candidate from employment, and any information provided will be treated confidentially and considered only for relevance to the role and the University’s safety obligations. Advertised: 22 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 08 Nov 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
