    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/949/cw/en/job/590231/lecturer-or-senior-lecturer-in-economics": {
      "title": "Lecturer or Senior Lecturer in Economics",
      "description": "Lecturer or Senior Lecturer in Economics Apply now Job no: 590231 Work type: Full-time Location: Melbourne - Burwood Categories: Business Based at our Melbourne, Burwood Campus + hybrid work arrangements Full-time and continuing, Generous relocation package and potential visa sponsorship for the right candidate Level B $122,391 - $144,759 + 17% Superannuation or Level C $149,230 - $171,602 + 17% Superannuation Why work with us? At Deakin you will have access to benefits such as a variety of leave options including generous parental leave and the ability to purchase additional leave; flexible working arrangements to help manage your work-life balance; ongoing learning and development opportunities to grow your career; an inclusive and supportive culture and environment to work in, both online and on campus. Who are we? Deakin is a cutting-edge public university headquartered in Victoria, revolutionising education with 61,000 students across our campuses: Melbourne Burwood, Geelong Waurn Ponds, Geelong Waterfront, Warrnambool, Deakin University Lancaster University Indonesia (DLI), GIFT City, India, and our vibrant online environment. We're proud to be a progressive and open-minded university, delivering the highest student satisfaction in Victoria and consistently ranked in the top 1% of the world's universities. As a Lecturer (Level B) or Senior Lecturer (Level C) in Economics, you will: Lead unit and course teaching teams, working collaboratively across the faculty to develop effective curricula and learning materials, review units and courses, and implement evidence-based refinements. Integrate industry, clinical and research practice into unit and course design to enhance student learning experiences, engagement, success and the relevance of learning resources. Lead the development and adoption of innovative, learner-centric and research-informed curriculum design across a range of levels and delivery modes, including the creation of inclusive and collaborative learning opportunities. Develop and moderate high-quality assessment tasks and rubrics at unit and course level, providing clear assessment criteria and timely feedback to demonstrate learning outcomes and strengthen student achievement. Foster a culture of collaboration and educational excellence by supporting students, mentoring colleagues, building teaching and learning capability, and contributing to pedagogical research and scholarly practice. To be successful, you’ll have: PhD in Economics or equivalent relevant qualifications and experience. The position is open to candidates from all fields of economics. However, preference will be given to candidates with strong expertise in macroeconomics, international trade, or microeconomic theory, and a developing or established research track record demonstrated through high-quality publications and/or success in securing external research funding. Excellent track record of high-quality teaching in both undergraduate and postgraduate levels, including experience in innovative curriculum development and in supervising Honours and/or HDR students. Capacity to contribute to research leadership, academic administration, and broader community engagement through impactful research. Excellent interpersonal skills and a proven ability to build effective working relationships with colleagues. Here's how to apply: Please submit your updated resume, a short cover letter and responses to the Key Selection Criteria. For enquiries regarding this position, please contact the Co-Chairs of the Recruitment Committee: Francisco Silva (Associate Professor), f.silva@deakin.edu.au Umair Khalil (Associate Professor), umair.khalil@deakin.edu.au For a copy of the position description, please see below: PD - Lecturer in Economics 590231.pdf PD - Senior Lecturer in Economics 590231.pdf Applications for this position close on 07 December 2026. Are You Ready? Deakin is a Victorian university with a global impact. We are an agile, dynamic, and innovative university committed to making a positive impact through our excellence in education, research and innovation and the contributions we make to the wider community. We understand that our reputation has been built on the dedication and expertise of our staff and we offer a dynamic and diverse working environment with opportunities to grow and develop careers. We believe that a progressive, thriving culture will ensure that people choose to come, and stay at Deakin and contribute to our ongoing success. We value diversity and aim to build an inclusive environment that champions, embraces and respects differences. We support and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, and people of all abilities, cultures, sexual orientation, and genders. Pre-employment safety and suitability checks: In accordance with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence, appointment to this role is subject to successful completion of relevant pre-employment checks, including Working With Children Checks and candidate gender-based violence declarations. Candidates may be asked to declare whether they have been investigated for an allegation of Gender‑based Violence, or determined to have engaged in conduct that constitutes Gender‑based Violence during the course of their previous employment, or otherwise in a legal process. A declaration is not required from individuals who have experienced gender‑based violence. A ‘Yes’ response does not automatically exclude a candidate from employment, and any information provided will be treated confidentially and considered only for relevance to the role and the University’s safety obligations. Advertised: 22 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 07 Dec 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/949/cw/en/job/590233/student-adviser": {
      "title": "Student Adviser",
      "description": "Student Adviser Apply now Job no: 590233 Work type: Continuing, Part-time Location: Melbourne - Burwood Categories: Administration, Student Services Based at our Melbourne, Burwood Campus + hybrid work arrangements 0.5FTE Part-time and continuing HEW 5 $86,744 - $97,290 + 17% Superannuation Please note hours of work are Monday & Tuesday - 10.20am to 7pm & Alternate Saturdays – 10.00am to 2.05pm Why work with us? At Deakin you will have access to benefits such as a variety of leave options including generous parental leave and the ability to purchase additional leave; flexible working arrangements to help manage your work-life balance; ongoing learning and development opportunities to grow your career; an inclusive and supportive culture and environment to work in, both online and on campus. As a Student Adviser, you will: Demonstrated ability to provide advice and support within an area of expertise, delivering high-quality service to stakeholders. Strong analytical skills with the ability to assess information, exercise judgement, and present clear recommendations to support decision-making. Proven organisational skills, with the capacity to plan, prioritise and manage competing workloads to achieve agreed outcomes. Well-developed stakeholder engagement skills, with the ability to build collaborative relationships and contribute positively to team objectives. Commitment to continuous improvement through identifying opportunities to enhance processes, systems and practices while meeting operational goals and service standards. To be successful, you’ll have: Relevant qualification and/or equivalent combination of education, training and demonstrated experience. Proven experience delivering advice, administrative support, or client-focused services. Well-developed communication skills with the ability to build and maintain effective stakeholder relationships. Demonstrated ability to interpret policies, procedures and guidelines, while coordinating competing priorities and workloads. Strong analytical and problem-solving skills with a commitment to continuous improvement and process enhancement initiatives. Here's how to apply: Please submit your updated resume and a short cover letter outlining your skills and experience. For a confidential discussion regarding this role, please contact Andrea Fyfe (Manager, Student Central -Contact Centre) via email at andrea.fyfe@deakin.edu.au The hours required to work are: Monday - 10.20am to 7pm Tuesday - 10.20am to 7pm Alternate Saturdays – 10.00am to 2.05pm For a copy of the position description, please see below: PD #590233 - Student Adviser Contact Centre.pdf Applications for this position close on Sunday 1 st November 2026. Are You Ready? Deakin is a Victorian university with a global impact. We are an agile, dynamic, and innovative university committed to making a positive impact through our excellence in education, research and innovation and the contributions we make to the wider community. We understand that our reputation has been built on the dedication and expertise of our staff and we offer a dynamic and diverse working environment with opportunities to grow and develop careers. We believe that a progressive, thriving culture will ensure that people choose to come, and stay at Deakin and contribute to our ongoing success. We value diversity and aim to build an inclusive environment that champions, embraces and respects differences. We support and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, and people of all abilities, cultures, sexual orientation, and genders. Pre-employment safety and suitability checks: In accordance with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence, appointment to this role is subject to successful completion of relevant pre-employment checks, including Working With Children Checks and candidate gender-based violence declarations. Candidates may be asked to declare whether they have been investigated for an allegation of Gender‑based Violence, or determined to have engaged in conduct that constitutes Gender‑based Violence during the course of their previous employment, or otherwise in a legal process. A declaration is not required from individuals who have experienced gender‑based violence. A ‘Yes’ response does not automatically exclude a candidate from employment, and any information provided will be treated confidentially and considered only for relevance to the role and the University’s safety obligations. Advertised: 22 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 01 Nov 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/949/cw/en/job/590193/lecturer-in-disability-and-inclusion-educationfocused": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Disability and Inclusion, Education-focused",
      "description": "Lecturer in Disability and Inclusion, Education-focused Apply now Job no: 590193 Work type: Continuing, Education-focused Academic, Part-time Location: Geelong - City, Melbourne - Burwood Categories: Health Based at our Melbourne, Burwood Campus and Geelong Waterfront Campuses Part-time (0.5FTE) and continuing in the School of Health and Social Development Level B $122,391 - $144,759 + 17% Superannuation (pro rata, part time) Applications are currently being accepted exclusively from our current and former sessional academic cohorts at Deakin. Applicants must have been engaged as a sessional academic at Deakin University within two years of the advertising date to be eligible to apply. Applications that do not meet these criteria will not be considered at this time. We are pleased to offer Deakin’s newest employment classification; the Education-focused Academic. These roles are tailored for academics dedicated to student-centred teaching, fostering a passion for teaching excellence and pioneering innovative educational practices. We invite applications from current and former sessional academics engaged with Deakin within the past two years. This presents a remarkable opportunity to contribute to shaping and co-designing this new academic career pathway, offering a genuine and meaningful option for those whose strengths lie in teaching and learning. The Faculty of Health is dedicated to being at the forefront of teaching, learning, creativity, social development, and groundbreaking research and discovery. We work closely with industry partners and government bodies to translate discoveries into real-word impact. This role will specifically be working within the Disability and Inclusion discipline within the School of Health and Social Development. About the role: The Education-focused Academic will lead unit teaching teams in UG and/or PG programs to improve the student learning experience and outcomes. Initiate, design and conduct productive, high-quality, scholarship and creative activities to enhance student experience and teaching learning outcomes This position is suited to a person with an emerging record and passion for learning and teaching, a keen interest in contributing to community engagement for teaching and employability. We encourage applications from people with lived experience of disability and inclusion. As a Lecturer in Disability and Inclusion, Education-focused, you will: Lead unit teaching teams and work collaboratively with other unit teaching teams in the discipline to develop effective unit curricula and learning resources Assist in applying industry, clinical and/or research practice into student learning experiences, success, engagement and resources. Maintain deep discipline knowledge which is applied to inform unit design. Design collaborative learning opportunities and develop effective assessment tasks at unit level. Provide academic support to students within units and guide students to appropriate support services where appropriate. To be successful, you’ll have: PhD in a relevant discipline and/or other relevant qualifications and experience Knowledge of disability and inclusion including international and national policy and practice Excellent record of scholarly learning and teaching in UG and/or PG programs, including innovative curriculum design Demonstrated passion for teaching and learning. Ability to deliver effective teaching practices to improve student success and engagement. Please note, teaching and collaboration across both Melbourne Burwood and Geelong Waterfront Campuses is essential. Here's how to apply: To be eligible to apply, you must currently be a sessional academic at Deakin or have been previously engaged as a sessional academic at Deakin within the last two years of the advertising date. To find out if you are eligible to apply, please contact Deakin Help on +61 3 5247 9999 or peopleconnect@deakin.edu.au Please submit your updated resume, a short cover letter and responses to the Key Selection Criteria. For a confidential discussion regarding this role, please contact Angela Dew (Professor, Disability and Inclusion) on angela.dew@deakin.edu.au . For a copy of the position description, please see below: PD - Lecturer in Disability and Inclusion, Education-focused, 590193.pdf Applications for this position close on Wednesday 28 October at 11:55pm. Are You Ready? Deakin is a Victorian university with a global impact. We are an agile, dynamic, and innovative university committed to making a positive impact through our excellence in education, research and innovation and the contributions we make to the wider community. We understand that our reputation has been built on the dedication and expertise of our staff and we offer a dynamic and diverse working environment with opportunities to grow and develop careers. We believe that a progressive, thriving culture will ensure that people choose to come, and stay at Deakin and contribute to our ongoing success. We value diversity and aim to build an inclusive environment that champions, embraces and respects differences. We support and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, and people of all abilities, cultures, sexual orientation, and genders. Pre-employment safety and suitability checks: In accordance with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence, appointment to this role is subject to successful completion of relevant pre-employment checks, including Working With Children Checks and candidate gender-based violence declarations. Candidates may be asked to declare whether they have been investigated for an allegation of Gender‑based Violence, or determined to have engaged in conduct that constitutes Gender‑based Violence during the course of their previous employment, or otherwise in a legal process. A declaration is not required from individuals who have experienced gender‑based violence. A ‘Yes’ response does not automatically exclude a candidate from employment, and any information provided will be treated confidentially and considered only for relevance to the role and the University’s safety obligations. Advertised: 21 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/949/cw/en/job/590205/service-improvement-lead": {
      "title": "Service Improvement Lead",
      "description": "Service Improvement Lead Apply now Job no: 590205 Work type: Part-time Location: Geelong - City, Melbourne - Burwood Categories: Library Services Based at our Melbourne, Burwood Campus or Geelong, Waterfront Campus + hybrid work arrangements Part-time (0.8FTE), and continuing HEW 7 $110,171 - $121,309 + 17% Superannuation About the Role: The Service Improvement Lead provides specialist expertise in service design, business process improvement and continuous improvement to enhance Library services and operations. Working collaboratively across the Library, the role identifies opportunities to improve service delivery, supports evidence-based decision-making and helps ensure services remain responsive, scalable and aligned with strategic priorities. As a key member of the team, you will work closely with service owners, stakeholders and delivery teams to drive measurable improvements, strengthen service management practices and support the Library's ongoing commitment to innovation and excellence. As a Service Improvement Lead, you will: Lead service improvement initiatives, driving enhancements to Library services, processes and user experiences. Analyse complex information and provide insights that support strategic and operational decision-making. Facilitate workshops, lead projects and coordinate improvement activities from planning through to implementation. Build strong partnerships with stakeholders to identify opportunities, influence outcomes and deliver shared goals. Contribute to the Library's service management maturity, continuous improvement culture, and digital and AI readiness. To be successful, you’ll have: Demonstrated experience in service improvement, business process improvement, service design or a related discipline. Strong analytical and problem-solving skills, with the ability to interpret complex information and identify practical solutions. Proven ability to influence, engage and collaborate with a diverse range of stakeholders. Experience leading projects, coordinating work programs or driving organisational change initiatives. A relevant degree and substantial subsequent experience, or an equivalent combination of education, expertise and professional experience. Here's how to apply: Please submit your updated resume and a short cover letter outlining your skills and experience. For a confidential discussion regarding this role, please contact Deb Sansom, Manager, Library Governance and Strategic Initiatives via deb.sansom@deakin.edu.au For a copy of the position description, please see below: Service Improvement Lead - HEW 7.pdf Applications for this position close on Tuesday 3rd November 2026. Benefits for working with us! At Deakin, we offer more than just a job - we offer a lifestyle. Across our campuses in Geelong, Melbourne, and regional Victoria, you’ll find everything you need to thrive: on-site childcare, gyms, medical centres, cafes, accessible public transport, and convenient parking. Enjoy flexible working arrangements that support your work-life balance, generous leave entitlements including extended parental leave and the option to purchase additional time off, plus a wide range of staff discounts on travel and more. Our health and wellbeing services, ongoing professional development opportunities, and inclusive, supportive culture make Deakin a place where you can grow - whether you're working remotely or immersed in the vibrant energy of campus life. Are You Ready? Deakin is a Victorian university with a global impact. We are an agile, dynamic, and innovative university committed to making a positive impact through our excellence in education, research and innovation and the contributions we make to the wider community. We understand that our reputation has been built on the dedication and expertise of our staff and we offer a dynamic and diverse working environment with opportunities to grow and develop careers. We believe that a progressive, thriving culture will ensure that people choose to come, and stay at Deakin and contribute to our ongoing success. We value diversity and aim to build an inclusive environment that champions, embraces and respects differences. We support and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, and people of all abilities, cultures, sexual orientation, and genders. Pre-employment safety and suitability checks: In accordance with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence, appointment to this role is subject to successful completion of relevant pre-employment checks, including Working With Children Checks and candidate gender-based violence declarations. Candidates may be asked to declare whether they have been investigated for an allegation of Gender‑based Violence, or determined to have engaged in conduct that constitutes Gender‑based Violence during the course of their previous employment, or otherwise in a legal process. A declaration is not required from individuals who have experienced gender‑based violence. A ‘Yes’ response does not automatically exclude a candidate from employment, and any information provided will be treated confidentially and considered only for relevance to the role and the University’s safety obligations. Advertised: 20 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 03 Nov 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/949/cw/en/job/589944/senior-adviser-work-integrated-learning": {
      "title": "Senior Adviser, Work Integrated Learning",
      "description": "Senior Adviser, Work Integrated Learning Apply now Job no: 589944 Work type: Full-time, Internal applicants only Location: Flexible, Geelong - Waurn Ponds, Melbourne - Burwood Categories: Administration, Customer Service • Geelong Waurn Ponds or Melbourne Burwood Campus + hybrid work arrangements – 2 days per week in the office • Full-time and fixed-term until late March 2027 • HEW 6 $100,128 - $108,130 + 17% Superannuation About the role: The Senior Adviser, Work Integrated Learning, supports the effective administration of work integrated learning (WIL) practices and processes for students, host organisations, and international and industry partners. This includes non-clinical placements and internships, compliance with university policy, study tours, and student engagement activities such as graduate employment initiatives and career events. In this role, you will: • Deliver programs and initiatives that increase student participation in work integrated learning (WIL) and provide valuable learning experiences. • Build and maintain productive relationships with internal and external stakeholders to develop placement opportunities and ensure students are appropriately placed, managed and supported in line with university policies and procedures. • Provide ethical, informed advice to students and stakeholders, offering practical recommendations and insights to address WIL-related issues. • Develop and implement accessible, practical solutions that respond to student and stakeholder needs, using effective communication and influencing skills to build understanding and support. • Maintain accurate records of WIL activities, including non-clinical placements, internships and career events. About you: We're looking for a collaborative and proactive professional with strong stakeholder engagement skills, sound judgement and an understanding of student advising, career education or international education. You will bring: • A relevant degree with subsequent experience, extensive specialist or broad technical/administrative knowledge, or an equivalent combination of experience, education and training. • Demonstrated ability to collaborate effectively with diverse internal and external stakeholders, clients and students. • Experience working within complex administrative structures, policies and procedures, preferably in higher education. • Experience in student advising, career education or international education. • Knowledge of the graduate employment landscape. Here's how to apply: Please submit your updated resume and a short cover letter outlining your skills and experience. For a confidential discussion regarding this role, please contact Raf Rahman, Manager, Work Integrated Learning, FOSEBE Partnerships & Engagement on raf.rahman@deakin.edu.au For a copy of the position description, please see below: PD - Senior Advisor, Work Intergrated Learning - HEW 6 (1).pdf Applications for this position close on Sunday 1st November at 11.55pm. Are You Ready? Deakin is a Victorian university with a global impact. We are an agile, dynamic, and innovative university committed to making a positive impact through our excellence in education, research and innovation and the contributions we make to the wider community. We understand that our reputation has been built on the dedication and expertise of our staff and we offer a dynamic and diverse working environment with opportunities to grow and develop careers. We believe that a progressive, thriving culture will ensure that people choose to come, and stay at Deakin and contribute to our ongoing success. We value diversity and aim to build an inclusive environment that champions, embraces and respects differences. We support and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, and people of all abilities, cultures, sexual orientation, and genders. Pre-employment safety and suitability checks: In accordance with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence, appointment to this role is subject to successful completion of relevant pre-employment checks, including Working With Children Checks and candidate gender-based violence declarations. Candidates may be asked to declare whether they have been investigated for an allegation of Gender-based Violence, or determined to have engaged in conduct that constitutes Gender-based Violence during the course of their previous employment, or otherwise in a legal process. A declaration is not required from individuals who have experienced gender-based violence. A ‘Yes’ response does not automatically exclude a candidate from employment, and any information provided will be treated confidentially and considered only for relevance to the role and the University’s safety obligations. Advertised: 19 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 01 Nov 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/949/cw/en/job/589775/night-support-officer-deakin-residential-services": {
      "title": "Night Support Officer, Deakin Residential Services",
      "description": "Night Support Officer, Deakin Residential Services Apply now Job no: 589775 Work type: Part-time Location: Geelong - Waurn Ponds Categories: Administration, Customer Service Based at our Geelong, Waurn Ponds Campus Part-time 0.35FTE and fixed-term until May 2027 HEW 4 $80,046 - $84,849 + 17% Superannuation Why this role matters This is an opportunity to play a vital role in creating a safe, welcoming and supportive residential community for Deakin students. As Night Support Officer, you will be a trusted point of contact during overnight hours, helping residents feel supported while ensuring the smooth operation of residential facilities at our Geelong Waurn Ponds campus. Working independently and as part of the broader Deakin Residential Services team, you will respond to enquiries, monitor facilities, assist with incident management, and provide administrative and operational support that contributes to a positive resident experience. Your calm, proactive approach and commitment to customer service will help maintain a secure and inclusive environment where students can thrive. This position is rostered from 5:00am to 8:00am, Monday to Thursday, and is integral to maintaining a safe, connected and well-supported residential environment for students. As a Night Support Officer you will: Assist residents, monitor facilities, respond to incidents, and support reception and short-stay accommodation services, ensuring consistent service delivery during night operations. Respond to enquiries, monitor security and maintenance concerns, and contribute to effective operational outcomes. Apply established processes and exercise judgement to deliver consistent outcomes Analyse information from available sources to support task completion and provide input to decision-making Contribute to team effectiveness through communication and collaboration To be successful, you’ll have: Diploma qualification with relevant experience; or certificate level qualification with post certificate work experience, or Post-Trades Certificate or Advanced Certificate or an equivalent combination of experience and/or education/training Experience in administrative or operational roles as well as working with systems and processes Ability to apply judgement to prioritise work and manage competing priorities Strong communication and interpersonal skills This position is designated a flexible ordinary hours span position due to operational needs. A 20% flexible loading applies to every hour worked outside the University Ordinary Hours of Work. Information about Flexible Ordinary Hours Span can be found in the Deakin Enterprise Agreement under Clause 43. Here's how to apply: Please submit your updated resume and a short cover letter outlining your skills and experience. For a confidential discussion regarding this role, please contact Eliza Hart (Senior Coordinator, Residential Experience) on e.hart@deakin.edu.au For a copy of the position description, please see below: PD - Night Support Officer 589775.pdf Applications for this position close on Thursday 12th November at 11:55pm. Benefits for working with us! At Deakin, we offer more than just a job. We offer a rewarding career in a flexible, inclusive and forward-thinking environment. Across our campuses in Geelong, Melbourne and regional Victoria, you'll enjoy flexible working arrangements that support work-life balance, generous leave provisions including extended parental leave and the option to purchase additional leave, and access to a range of professional development opportunities to help you grow your career. Are You Ready? Deakin is a Victorian university with a global impact. We are an agile, dynamic, and innovative university committed to making a positive impact through our excellence in education, research and innovation and the contributions we make to the wider community. We understand that our reputation has been built on the dedication and expertise of our staff and we offer a dynamic and diverse working environment with opportunities to grow and develop careers. We believe that a progressive, thriving culture will ensure that people choose to come, and stay at Deakin and contribute to our ongoing success. We value diversity and aim to build an inclusive environment that champions, embraces and respects differences. We support and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, and people of all abilities, cultures, sexual orientation, and genders. Pre-employment safety and suitability checks: In accordance with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence, appointment to this role is subject to successful completion of relevant pre-employment checks, including Working With Children Checks and candidate gender-based violence declarations. Candidates may be asked to declare whether they have been investigated for an allegation of Gender‑based Violence, or determined to have engaged in conduct that constitutes Gender‑based Violence during the course of their previous employment, or otherwise in a legal process. A declaration is not required from individuals who have experienced gender‑based violence. A ‘Yes’ response does not automatically exclude a candidate from employment, and any information provided will be treated confidentially and considered only for relevance to the role and the University’s safety obligations. Advertised: 29 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/949/cw/en/job/590462/teaching-fellow-clinical-skills": {
      "title": "Teaching Fellow, Clinical Skills",
      "description": "Teaching Fellow, Clinical Skills Apply now Job no: 590462 Work type: Fixed-term, Part-time Location: Geelong - Waurn Ponds Categories: Health • Based at our Geelong, Waurn Ponds Campus • 2 X 0.2 FTE and 3 X 0.25 FTE part-time, fixed term opportunities from 11 January 2027 until 8th of January 2029 • Level B $122,391 - $144,759 + 17% Superannuation (pro rata, part time) About the role: Join the school of Medicine Clinical Practice Team as a Teaching Fellow. We have multiple positions providing exciting opportunities for enthusiastic educators to contribute to the development of the next generation of medical practitioners. Successful applicants will guide, develop, and deliver high-quality teaching and learning activities that foster student engagement and support an outstanding learning experience. Teaching Fellows will contribute to curriculum delivery and assessment across clinical practice communication skills, procedural skills, history taking and clinical examination skills. They will also support student learning and work collaboratively with academic and clinical colleagues to maintain a rich and supportive educational environment. The Teaching Fellow positions will primarily focus on teaching, learning, assessment and associated service tasks and will be required to have availability on primarily Tuesdays (Year 2 teaching) and Thursdays (Year 1 teaching) and the occasional Wednesdays and Fridays. As a Teaching Fellow, Clinical Skills, you will: Deliver effective teaching practices to achieve student learning experiences to support success and engagement. Apply industry, clinical and/or research practice to student learning experiences, success, engagement and resources. Contribute to and work collaboratively with other unit teaching teams in the course to develop effective unit curricula, clinical/professional education experiences and learning resources. Contribute to the review of units and courses and implement refinements. To be successful, you’ll have: A PhD in a related field or equivalent professional experience (for example, in clinical health) and standing in a relevant field. Excellent record of scholarly learning and teaching in UG and/or PG programs Ability to deliver effective teaching practices to improve student success and engagement Excellent interpersonal skills and a proven ability to establish good working relationships with colleagues. Demonstrated capacity and passion for teaching and facilitation of student learning. This position requires the incumbent to hold a current APHRA Registration. Special Requirements: This position requires the incumbent to hold a current National Police Record Check Here's how to apply: Applicants must have full Australian working rights. Visa sponsorship is not available for this position Please submit your updated resume, a short cover letter highlighting teaching and clinical experience and responses to the Key Selection Criteria. Please include your fractional preference. For a confidential discussion regarding this role, please contact the Clinical Practice Theme Leader; Kellie Britt, Senior Lecturer, Clinical Medicine on kellie.britt@deakin.edu.au For a copy of the position description, please see below: PD - Teaching Fellow, Clinical Skills 590462.pdf Applications for this position close at 11.55pm on Monday 16 November 2026. Who are we? Deakin is a cutting-edge public university headquartered in Victoria, revolutionising education with 61,000 students across our campuses: Melbourne Burwood, Geelong Waurn Ponds, Geelong Waterfront, Warrnambool, Deakin University Lancaster University Indonesia (DLI), GIFT City, India, and our vibrant online environment. We're proud to be a progressive and open-minded university, delivering the highest student satisfaction in Victoria and consistently ranked in the top 1% of the world's universities. Are You Ready? Deakin is a Victorian university with a global impact. We are an agile, dynamic, and innovative university committed to making a positive impact through our excellence in education, research and innovation and the contributions we make to the wider community. We understand that our reputation has been built on the dedication and expertise of our staff and we offer a dynamic and diverse working environment with opportunities to grow and develop careers. We believe that a progressive, thriving culture will ensure that people choose to come, and stay at Deakin and contribute to our ongoing success. We value diversity and aim to build an inclusive environment that champions, embraces and respects differences. We support and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, and people of all abilities, cultures, sexual orientation, and genders. Pre-employment safety and suitability checks: In accordance with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence, appointment to this role is subject to successful completion of relevant pre-employment checks, including Working With Children Checks and candidate gender-based violence declarations. Candidates may be asked to declare whether they have been investigated for an allegation of Gender‑based Violence, or determined to have engaged in conduct that constitutes Gender‑based Violence during the course of their previous employment, or otherwise in a legal process. A declaration is not required from individuals who have experienced gender‑based violence. A ‘Yes’ response does not automatically exclude a candidate from employment, and any information provided will be treated confidentially and considered only for relevance to the role and the University’s safety obligations. Advertised: 30 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 16 Nov 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    }
